# TODO - Guest runtime and allocator

## Objective

Implement startup, calling convention, frames, allocation, streams, integer
helpers, strings, deterministic math helpers, scheduling primitives, and other
runtime facilities as code that ultimately executes under Malbolge semantics.

## Acceptance

- Allocation, streams, arithmetic/math helpers, calling-convention support,
  and other runtime facilities have deterministic guest-owned semantics and
  freestanding implementations that do not delegate guest computation to hidden
  host callbacks.
- Fundamental compiler intrinsics such as byte output have explicit versioned
  guest-runtime contracts suitable for downstream lowering. Native debug
  adapters may mirror them but cannot define their semantics.
- Executable ternary/Malbolge realization is downstream completion evidence for
  `ternary-machine-lowering`; this lane must provide the stable runtime semantic
  identities and implementation inputs that stage consumes unchanged.
- Accepted and rejected C fixtures exercise the boundary, and diagnostics
  identify the unsupported construct/profile requirement at source level.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `tools/tidy/`, `src/runtime/`,
  `docs/technical/specification/`, `tests/tidy/`, `tests/runtime/`, and
  `tests/test_guest_runtime_c.py`.
- Required evidence: accepted/rejected source fixtures, source-located
  diagnostics, runtime semantic/conformance fixtures, and regression tests that
  prove no host callback is required to define guest computation.
- Implemented contract-authority evidence: tracked
  `guest-runtime-v1.json` owns runtime-specific status values, heap metadata and
  policy, startup behavior, frame-authority reference, byte intrinsic
  identities,
  and the no-host-fallback rule. `tests/test_guest_runtime_authority.py` ties
  the
  C projection back to that contract, `c-abi-v1.json`, current `malbolge.json`,
  and staged `c-libc-v1.json` availability.
- Implemented runtime-core evidence: governed `src/runtime/guest-runtime/`
  owns a version-one C contract plus freestanding heap and byte-stream domains.
  The heap uses 16-byte-aligned first-fit allocation with canonical 16-byte
  little-endian metadata, checked 32-bit extents, split/coalesce/tail reclaim,
  deterministic null for zero-size allocation, zeroed allocation, and resize
  with old-allocation preservation on exhaustion. Every public heap mutation
  preflights the entire canonical chain. Invalid later metadata rejects before
  earlier state or result publication changes.
- Implemented byte-stream evidence: profile input words `0..255` map to C byte
  values, profile EOF `4,782,968` maps to `-1`, impossible intermediate words
  fail explicitly, and output consumes the low eight bits without host stdio.
- Implemented conformance evidence: `tests/test_guest_runtime_c.py` compiles the
  independent C vectors with strict warnings for i686, x86-64, and AArch64
  Windows ABIs, then executes the native vector suite. The vectors lock exact
  metadata bytes, alignment, reuse, split/coalesce, calloc overflow/zeroing,
  realloc in-place/tail-growth/move/failure preservation, corruption rejection,
  late-corruption nonmutation, and I/O. A deterministic 4,000-operation stress
  run validates payloads plus an
  independently parsed raw metadata chain after every operation. ASan/UBSan and
  path-sensitive Clang analysis are clean. Cross-ABI object-symbol checks
  prevent compiler-injected library helpers from becoming hidden runtime

  dependencies.
- Implemented frame evidence: the runtime consumes the canonical
  `malbolge-c32-v1` 32-byte hidden frame layout directly from ABI authority. Its
  codec validates 16-byte frame extent, required argument-block pointer, and
  zero v1 flags; encoding is little-endian and validation-gated, while decoding
  stages fields before publication. The C vector locks exact frame bytes and
  invalid-frame nonpublication.
- Implemented startup/allocation-wrapper evidence: guest startup owns a
  one-time heap binder; pre-bind allocation fails explicitly, invalid binding
  leaves the runtime uninitialized, and successful binding cannot be replaced.
  The four public libc allocation wrappers delegate only to guest-runtime
  symbols. They intentionally remain unavailable in `malbolge-libc-v1` until
  compiler-generated startup wiring proves the bind before user code.
- Implemented byte-wrapper evidence: declaration-only intrinsic symbols own
  profile input-word and output-byte effects; `getchar`/`putchar` wrappers use
  only those symbols plus pure runtime mapping. Independent test intrinsics
  prove byte, EOF, impossible-input, low-eight-bit, and return-value behavior.
  The routines remain unavailable until `ternary-machine-lowering` realizes the
  intrinsic symbols as selected-profile `/` and `<` operations.
- Implemented formatting evidence: `guest_format.h`/`format.c` provide a
  private typed substrate for integer bases 2/8/10/16, strings, characters,
  width/precision/sign/prefix rules, bounded writes, and would-have-written
  counting. `guest_format_parse.h`/`format_parse.c` add a separate C23 narrow
  tokenizer for literals, flags, literal/dynamic width and precision, classic
  plus `wN`/`wfN` length modifiers, `%b`/`%B`, floating tags, pointer/count
  tags,
  and fail-closed decimal overflow/incomplete directives without partial token
  publication. Directive admission additionally enforces conversion/length/
  precision/flag relationships and guest specific-width support at 8/16/32/64
  bits. The canonical promoted-block cursor decodes 32/64/128-bit scalar and
  pointer representations with guest natural alignment; transactional resolution
  consumes dynamic fields plus the main promoted argument with rollback on late
  failure. Scalar execution covers `d/i/u/o/x/X/b/B/c/p/%`, including explicit

  post-promotion narrowing. `%p` deterministically prints the guest object-
  pointer encoding (`0` for null; lowercase `0x...` for non-null) without a
  host address or dereference. `%lc` fails closed pending `wint_t` authority.
  Cross-ABI checks show no callable host formatting dependency.
- Public formatting remains gated: `snprintf`/`vsnprintf` remain unavailable
  because compiler lowering has not yet bridged source `va_list` state into the
  canonical cursor and floating conversion execution is intentionally not
  implemented yet.
- Implemented guest-memory formatting evidence:
  `guest_format_memory.h`/`format_memory.c` execute narrow `%s` and integer `%n`
  over one caller-proven live guest object. The boundary decodes the ABI's
  offset-plus-one pointer representation, proves complete range and `%n`
  alignment before publication, and bounds `%s` reads by object extent or
  precision. Representable `%n` counts are stored in canonical little-endian
  ABI widths. Null, out-of-object, unterminated, misaligned, and count-overflow
  cases fail without sink or guest-object mutation; `%ls` remains fail-closed.
- Implemented hexadecimal-formatting evidence:
  `guest_format_float.h`/`format_float.c` execute binary64 `%a`/`%A` and
  binary128 `%La`/`%LA` from raw promoted bits without host floating arithmetic
  or `__int128`. Nonzero values use normalized-leading-one style, including
  subnormals through exponents `-1074` and `-16494`. Missing precision trims
  exact trailing hex zeros; smaller precision rounds nibbles nearest-ties-even
  and larger precision appends virtual zeros without proportional discarded
  output work. Vectors lock extremes, special values, padding, ties, truncation,
  and fail-closed decimal/unresolved fields.
- Implemented binary64 exact-decimal substrate evidence:
  `guest_decimal_exact.h`/`format_decimal_exact.c` convert every finite raw F64
  magnitude into canonical decimal digits times a signed power of ten. The
  worst exact numerator is bounded at 767 digits; fixed base-10000 scratch uses
  only 32-bit multiply/carry operations. Manual extremes plus a deterministic
  256-pattern Python big-integer differential prove exact digits and shift.
  Decimal `%e/%f/%g` spelling remains gated above this reusable substrate.
- Implemented exact-math evidence: `fabs`, `floor`, `ceil`, and `trunc` are
  ordinary guest C over ABI-fixed binary64 representation, preserve signed-zero
  and infinity semantics, map every NaN to the canonical quiet payload-zero NaN,
  and execute in the no-CRT harness. A fixed-seed 274-pattern rational
  differential independently verifies exact result bits. Canonical `sqrt` now
  uses a representation-only restoring integer algorithm with ABI-fixed
  nearest-ties-even rounding; a separate 532-pattern arbitrary-precision
  `isqrt` differential, no-CRT execution, and cross-ABI symbol checks prove its
  result bits and lack of host/compiler helper fallback. `sin`, `cos`, and
  `atan2` remain unavailable because their correctly-rounded transcendental
  algorithms are not yet proven.
- Resolved integer/scheduling boundary: version one introduces no separate
  guest helper ABI for ordinary integer arithmetic or scheduling. Integer
  operations are explicit typed-IR semantics consumed by lane 9; the selected
  target profile is sequential and exposes no guest thread/scheduler API. A
  future guest concurrency surface requires a new versioned contract rather than
  hidden host scheduling.
- Resolved integration boundary: allocation startup binding and byte-I/O target
  effects are lane-9 realization work. This lane supplies their stable binder,
  wrapper, frame, and intrinsic identities unchanged and does not make itself
  depend on its downstream consumer merely to flip source availability.
- Remaining lane-8 algorithm work: decimal floating formatting plus
  correctly-rounded `sin`, `cos`, and `atan2`. Source `va_list` bridging
  remains downstream
  compiler-lowering integration work over the implemented canonical cursor.
  Wide `%ls` remains unavailable because version one has no completed wide-
  character formatting execution policy.
  These routines remain source-unavailable, so their absence
  does not invalidate currently accepted C; expanding availability must preserve
  that lowerability invariant.
- Implemented source-gate evidence: dedicated `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc`,
  `free`, `getchar`, `putchar`, `snprintf`, and `vsnprintf` rejected fixtures
  prove implementation substrate does not prematurely change canonical
  availability; each still produces an exact `MALBOLGE-LIBC-001` diagnostic at
  its call site.
- Dependency-boundary evidence: this lane intentionally stops before target
  lowering. `ternary-machine-lowering` depends on these runtime identities and
  owns their executable ternary/Malbolge realization, avoiding a planning cycle.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `supported-libc-contract`,
  `safe-rust-malbolge-vm`.
